==English==
{{was wotd|2007|July|11}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*weyd-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|invidiōsus}}, from {{m|la|invidia||envy, ill will}}, from {{m|la|in-||upon}} + {{m|la|videō||I see}}. {{doublet|en|envious}}, from Old French.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈvɪdi.əs/}}
* {{a|US}} {{audio|en|en-us-invidious.ogg}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪdiəs|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Causing [[ill will]], [[envy]], or [[offense]].
#* {{RQ:Adventurer|number=81|page=62|date=August 14, 1753|passage=To think highly of ourselves in comparison with others, to assume by our own authority that precedence which none is willing to grant, must be always '''invidious''' and offensive; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Pickwick Papers|chapter=XXXII|passage=‘I didn’t make use of the word in any '''invidious''' sense, ma’am,’ replied Mr. Benjamin Allen, growing somewhat uneasy on his own account.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Far from the Madding Crowd|passage={{...|Gabriel hoped that the whole truth of the matter might not be published till at any rate the girl had been in her grave for a few days,}} when the interposing barriers of earth and time, and a sense that the events had been somewhat shut into oblivion, would deaden the sting that revelation and '''invidious''' remark would have for Bathsheba just now.}}
#* {{RQ:James Roderick Hudson|passage=He lingered on the bridges at sunset, and knew that the light was enchanting and the mountains divine, but there seemed to be something horribly '''invidious''' and unwelcome in the fact.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1951| author=Winston Churchill| title=Closing the Ring| page=339| publisher=Boston: Houghton Mifflin| passage=I should be very reluctant, as head of His Majesty's Government, to place such an '''invidious''' responsibility upon a British officer.}}
# {{lb|en|of a distinction}} [[offensive|Offensively]] or [[unfair]]ly [[discriminate|discriminating]].
#* {{RQ:Thoreau Walden|passage=But the rich man—not to make any '''invidious''' comparison—is always sold to the institution which makes him rich.}}
#* {{RQ:London John Barleycorn|chapter=XVII|passage=There were so many drinks to be drunk, and as the warm magic poured through our veins and mellowed our voices and affections we knew it was no time to make '''invidious''' distinctions—to drink with this shipmate and to decline to drink with that shipmate.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[envious|Envious]], [[jealous]].
# [[detestable|Detestable]], [[hateful]], or [[odious]]. {{n-g|(Often used in cases of perceived unfairness, or when facing a difficult situation or choice — especially in the phrase ''invidious position''.)}}
#: {{ux|en|The sales representative was in an '''invidious''' position:  she could either get to the meeting on time but with nothing to show the client, or pick up the product samples while the client was kept waiting.  Neither one would make a favourable impression!}}

